      "evidence": "In the spring of my senior year of high school, my father's hours at the warehouse were cut in half, and my family decided that I would take the early shift at a neighborhood bakery to cover our rent gap. I had already been accepted to college, and I refused to give that up. That meant shaping dough from four to nine, driving straight to an eight-thirty class I was always late for, and studying in the twenty minutes between the end of my shift and my next lecture. By October I had failed my first chemistry exam, and I seriously considered dropping out.\n\nWhat kept me going was a set of small, stubborn habits. I asked my chemistry professor whether I could take a later lab section, and she moved me without hesitation. I recorded my lecture notes as audio and listened to them while I kneaded. I told my manager the truth about my schedule, and he let me leave fifteen minutes early on exam days if I came in fifteen minutes early. By the end of the year I had raised my chemistry grade to a B-plus and had not missed a single shift.\n\nMy grandmother, who had worked night shifts in a laundry for thirty years, called me every Sunday and quizzed me on the periodic table over the phone, even though she had never studied chemistry herself. In return I taught her how to video call my cousins abroad, and our Sunday calls became the best hour of both our weeks.\n\nThat year changed what I want to do with my life. I used to think of perseverance as a personality trait that some people simply have. Now I see it as a system: asking for help early, being honest about limits, and building routines that survive a bad week. I want to become a pharmacist, and I plan to work in a community pharmacy where many customers, like my family, juggle shift work and health.",
